 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186269229 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ 