an find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{54}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 27\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{54}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186269232 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269232 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269233 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186269233 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}})≈{24.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}})≈{24.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186269234 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186269234 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{8} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186269235 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269235 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get $3{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get $3{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269238 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269238 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,6\\,|=6\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,6\\,|=6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269247 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186269247 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [:db/cas 17592186269248 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-6\\,|=6\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-6\\,|=6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269249 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269250 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269251 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression f